    "text": "Computational thinking means approaching a problem the way a computer scientist does — analysing it logically so that a computer (or a person following precise steps) could solve it. It is NOT \"thinking like a computer\"; it is thinking about problems in a way that makes them solvable by computers. Topic 1 of the 4CP0 specification is built on three connected ideas: Technique One-line definition Question it answers Decomposition Breaking a large problem into smaller, more manageable sub-problems \"What separate parts make up this problem?\" Abstraction Removing or hiding unnecessary detail so only the essential features remain \"What actually matters here, and what can I ignore?\" Algorithm design Writing a precise sequence of steps that solves each sub-problem (Chapter 2) \"What exact steps solve it?\" 🖼️ Image coming soon Figure 1.1 Computational thinking: a large messy problem is decomposed into sub-problems, abstraction strips away irrelevant detail, and algorithms are designed for what remains. assets/images/ch1-computational-thinking.png 📋 SPEC 1.2.1 Understand the benefit of using decomposition and abstraction to model aspects of the real world and analyse problems and systems. 💡 Why This Topic Opens the Course Every other topic depends on these two ideas. Algorithms (Topic 1.2) are designed for the sub-problems decomposition produces. Subprograms in Python (Topic 6) are decomposition written as code. Even binary itself is an abstraction — a voltage in a wire is \"abstracted\" to the symbol 1 or 0."
