    "heading": "2. Decomposition — Breaking Problems Down",
    "id": "h-3",
    "text": "Decomposition is breaking a large, complex problem down into smaller, more manageable sub-problems. Each sub-problem can then be understood, solved and tested separately — and, if it is still too big, decomposed again. When the solution is eventually programmed, each sub-problem typically becomes a subprogram (a function or procedure). 2.1 Worked Example — Decomposing a Computer Game Suppose you are asked to write a simple arcade game. \"Write a game\" is far too big to tackle in one go. Decomposed, it becomes a set of clear, separate jobs: PROBLEM: Build an arcade game ├── 1. Display the game world (draw background, sprites, score) ├── 2. Handle player input (keyboard / controller presses) ├── 3. Move the player's character ├── 4. Move the enemies │ ├── 4a. Decide each enemy's direction │ └── 4b. Detect collisions with walls ├── 5. Detect collisions between player and enemies ├── 6. Update and display the score └── 7. Check for game over and show the final screen Notice that sub-problem 4 was still too large, so it was decomposed again. You stop decomposing when each sub-problem is small enough to design an algorithm for directly. 2.2 The Four Benefits of Decomposition Benefit Why it helps Exam-ready phrasing Simpler problems Small sub-problems are easier to understand and solve than one huge problem \"Each sub-problem is smaller and easier to solve than the whole problem\" Teamwork Different people (or teams) can work on different sub-problems at the same time \"Sub-problems can be developed in parallel by different programmers, so the solution is finished sooner\" Reuse A solved sub-problem (e.g. \"validate a date\") may already exist or can be reused in other projects \"Existing/library solutions to sub-problems can be reused, saving development time\" Easier testing & debugging Each part can be tested on its own, so errors are found and isolated quickly \"Each sub-problem can be tested separately, making errors easier to locate and fix\" ⚡ EXAM TIP When a question asks for benefits of decomposition, never just write \"it makes the problem easier\". Develop the point: easier because each sub-problem is smaller, can be solved/tested independently, and can be shared across a team. The development is what earns the second mark. 2.3 Decomposition → Subprograms Decomposition is not just a paper exercise — it maps directly onto code. Each sub-problem becomes a named subprogram, and a short main program calls them in order: # Each sub-problem from the game becomes a subprogram def draw_world(): ... # sub-problem 1 def handle_input(): ... # sub-problem 2 def move_enemies(): ... # sub-problem 4 def check_collisions(): ... # sub-problem 5 # Main game loop — reads like the decomposition itself while not game_over: handle_input() move_enemies() check_collisions() draw_world() 📌 Link to Spec 1.2.2 The benefits of subprograms mirror the benefits of decomposition: code is easier to read, write, test and debug; subprograms can be reused; and teams can write different subprograms simultaneously. You will meet subprograms properly in the Python chapters — for now, remember that decomposition is the planning step and subprograms are the coding step ."
