    "heading": "3. Abstraction — Removing Unnecessary Detail",
    "id": "h-7",
    "text": "Abstraction is removing or hiding unnecessary detail so that you can concentrate on the essential features of a problem. The result is a simplified model of the real world that contains only what is needed for the task. What counts as \"unnecessary\" depends entirely on the purpose of the model. 3.1 The Classic Example — The London Underground Map 🖼️ Image coming soon Figure 1.2 Abstraction in action: a geographically accurate map of London (left) versus the schematic Underground map (right), which keeps only stations, lines and interchanges. assets/images/ch1-tube-map-abstraction.png The London Underground map (designed by Harry Beck in 1931) is the example almost every examiner expects. A passenger planning a journey needs to know which line to take, where to change, and how many stops remain. They do NOT need real distances, street layouts or the depth of tunnels — so the map removes all of that: KEPT (essential detail) REMOVED (unnecessary detail) Station names and their order along each line Real geographical distances between stations Which line each station is on (colour-coded) The actual curved routes the tunnels take Interchange stations where passengers can change lines Streets, buildings and surface geography The River Thames (a rough position guide) Depth of tunnels, engineering details, timetables The abstracted map is less accurate but more useful — because it matches the passenger's purpose. That trade-off is the heart of abstraction. 3.2 Abstraction in Games and Models A computer game is an abstraction of the real world. A football game models the pitch, the ball, player positions and the score — the essentials of football. It does not model the grass growing, the weather in neighbouring towns, or each spectator's thoughts, because none of that affects gameplay. Similarly: Model Purpose Detail kept Detail removed Driving game Fun, believable driving Speed, steering, track layout, collisions Engine chemistry, tyre temperature physics, fuel economy Weather forecast model Predict tomorrow's weather Air pressure, temperature, wind, humidity readings Individual clouds, leaves blowing, exact raindrop positions School timetable system Schedule lessons without clashes Teachers, classes, rooms, periods, subjects Room colour, what teachers wear, the content of each lesson Flight simulator Train pilots safely Cockpit controls, physics of lift/drag, instrument readings Passengers' conversations, in-flight meals, cabin decor 3.3 Variables Are Abstractions Too When you write a program, every variable is an abstraction. A real player in a quiz has a face, a voice and a history — but if the program only needs to track their progress, all of that is abstracted away to a few values: # A real-world player abstracted to just the data the program needs player_name = \"Asha\" # string — identifies the player score = 0 # integer — tracks progress lives = 3 # integer — remaining attempts is_playing = True # Boolean — current state # A whole class of 30 real students abstracted to a list of marks marks = [56, 72, 38, 91, 64] ⚡ EXAM TIP \"State what is meant by abstraction\" is a bankable 1-mark question. Learn the exact wording: \"removing or hiding unnecessary detail so that the essential features of a problem can be focused on\" . For a second mark, apply it: say WHICH detail is removed in the given scenario and WHY it is not needed for the purpose."
