    "heading": "1. What Is an Algorithm?",
    "id": "h-2",
    "text": "An algorithm is a precise, step-by-step sequence of instructions that solves a problem or performs a task. Every step must be unambiguous (only one possible meaning), the steps must be in a logical order, and the algorithm must eventually finish. A recipe, the instructions for assembling flat-pack furniture, and a program that finds your highest test score are all algorithms. Property What it means If it's missing… Precise / unambiguous Each step has exactly one interpretation \"Add some flour\" — a computer cannot guess how much Ordered Steps happen in a defined sequence Baking the cake before mixing the ingredients fails Finite The algorithm must terminate A loop that never ends never produces an answer Effective It actually solves the stated problem for all valid inputs An algorithm that only works for some inputs has a logic error Algorithms are used in two directions in this course: designing solutions to new problems, and reading existing algorithms to work out what they do (often using a trace table). Paper 1 tests both. 💡 Three Ways to Express the SAME Algorithm 1. Written description: numbered steps in English — good for explaining to people. 2. Flowchart: standard symbols joined by arrows — good for visualising decisions and loops. 3. Program code: precise enough for a computer to execute — in this course, Python. The logic is identical in all three; only the notation changes. Exam questions frequently give you one form and ask you to interpret or complete another."
