    "text": "📋 SPEC — Appendix 5: Pseudocode command set Questions in the written examination that involve code use the Edexcel pseudocode command set for clarity and consistency. You may answer questions using any valid method — pseudocode, Python, C#, Java or clear structured English — but you must be able to READ Edexcel pseudocode fluently. Edexcel publishes a defined pseudocode in the specification (Appendix 5). Learn to read every construct below — exam algorithms are written in it. Construct Edexcel pseudocode Python equivalent Assignment SET Counter TO 0 counter = 0 Output SEND 'Hello' TO DISPLAY print(\"Hello\") Input RECEIVE age FROM (INTEGER) KEYBOARD age = int(input()) Selection IF Answer = 10 THEN … ELSE … END IF if answer == 10: … else: … Pre-condition loop WHILE Flag = 0 DO … END WHILE while flag == 0: … Post-condition loop REPEAT … UNTIL Go = 10 (no direct equivalent — loop runs at least once) Count loop FOR Index FROM 1 TO 10 DO … END FOR for index in range(1, 11): … For-each loop FOR EACH Word FROM WordsArray DO … END FOREACH for word in words: … Arrays SET ArrayValues TO [1, 2, 3] — indices start at 0 values = [1, 2, 3] Subprograms PROCEDURE … END PROCEDURE · FUNCTION … RETURN … END FUNCTION def name(params): … return … Operators MOD (remainder), DIV (integer division), ^ (power), & (append/join) % , // , ** , + Other LENGTH() , RANDOM(n) , CONST , # comment len() , random.randint(0,n) , constants by convention, # ⚡ EXAM TIP You will not lose marks for writing answers in real Python (or C#/Java) — the spec says \"students may answer questions using any valid method\". But algorithms IN the paper are printed in Edexcel pseudocode, so practise reading SET / SEND / RECEIVE until they feel as natural as Python. Helpfully, a copy of the pseudocode command set is enclosed with the real Paper 1 — you can refer to it during the exam, so fluency in reading it matters more than memorising it. 2.1 Variables and Assignment A variable is a named location in memory that stores a value which can change while the program runs. Assignment uses = and means \"store the value on the right in the variable named on the left\" — it is NOT the maths equals sign. score = 0 # create variable score, store 0 in it score = score + 1 # take the current value (0), add 1, store the result (1) name = input(\"Enter your name: \") # input is always a string age = int(input(\"Enter your age: \")) # convert the string to an integer print(\"Hello\", name) # output to the screen ⚡ EXAM TIP x = x + 1 confuses many candidates. Read it right-to-left: \"work out x + 1 using the CURRENT value of x, then store the answer back in x.\" In a trace table, this line creates a NEW value in the x column."
