    "heading": "3. Flowcharts — Symbols and Construction",
    "id": "h-5",
    "text": "A flowchart shows an algorithm as standard symbols connected by arrows (flow lines). You must recognise and use these symbols exactly — examiners penalise the wrong shape. 🖼️ Image coming soon Figure 2.1 The five flowchart symbols required by 4CP0: oval terminator, rectangle process, parallelogram input/output, diamond decision and the double-barred subprogram box, connected by flow-line arrows. assets/images/ch2-flowchart-symbols.png Symbol Shape Used for Example label Terminator Oval / rounded rectangle The start and end of the algorithm (every flowchart has exactly one START) START · STOP Process Rectangle A calculation or assignment — anything that changes a value total = total + mark Input / Output Parallelogram Getting data from the user or displaying results INPUT mark · OUTPUT total Decision Diamond A yes/no question — the flow splits into two labelled branches mark > highest? Subprogram Rectangle with double side bars A call to a separately defined subprogram (links to decomposition) validate(mark) Flow line Arrow Shows the order in which symbols are followed — 3.1 Building a Flowchart — Decision and Loop Loops in flowcharts are made by drawing a flow line from a decision diamond back up to an earlier symbol. Here is the structure for a program that keeps asking for a password until it is correct, shown as a written description and as the equivalent Python: WRITTEN DESCRIPTION FLOWCHART STRUCTURE 1. Start. START (oval) 2. Ask the user for the password. ↓ 3. Is it equal to \"open123\"? INPUT pword (parallelogram) - No → go back to step 2. ↓ - Yes → continue. pword == \"open123\"? (diamond) 4. Display \"Hello\". ├─ No → arrow loops back to INPUT 5. Stop. └─ Yes ↓ OUTPUT \"Hello\" (parallelogram) ↓ STOP (oval) # The same algorithm in Python pword = input(\"Password? \") while pword != \"open123\": pword = input(\"Password? \") print(\"Hello\") ⚡ EXAM TIP — Drawing Flowcharts Marks are routinely lost for: missing START/STOP terminators, decision diamonds without BOTH branches labelled (Yes/No or True/False), arrows missing their direction, and input written inside a rectangle. Check all four before moving on. If a question asks you to COMPLETE a flowchart, the empty shapes tell you what kind of statement belongs in each gap."
