    "heading": "5. Trace Tables — Following an Algorithm by Hand",
    "id": "h-10",
    "text": "A trace table records the value of every variable, condition and output line by line as an algorithm executes. It is the standard tool for predicting output, understanding unfamiliar code and hunting down logic errors. One column per variable (plus columns for any condition tested and for output); a new entry each time something changes. 5.1 Worked Example — Tracing a Total/Counter Loop 1 total = 0 2 count = 1 3 while count <= 4: 4 total = total + count 5 count = count + 1 6 print(total) Line total count count <= 4? Output 1 0 2 1 3 True 4 1 5 2 3 True 4 3 5 3 3 True 4 6 5 4 3 True 4 10 5 5 3 False 6 10 The trace shows the loop body runs exactly four times and the final output is 10 (1+2+3+4). Purpose of the algorithm: it calculates and outputs the sum of the whole numbers from 1 to 4. 5.2 Worked Example — Tracing a Maximum-Finding Loop 1 nums = [3, 8, 5, 11, 2] 2 highest = nums[0] 3 for i in range(1, 5): # i takes the values 1, 2, 3, 4 4 if nums[i] > highest: 5 highest = nums[i] 6 print(highest) i nums[i] nums[i] > highest? highest Output — — — 3 1 8 True 8 2 5 False 8 3 11 True 11 4 2 False 11 — — — 11 11 🖼️ Image coming soon Figure 2.2 A trace table being filled in: one column per variable and condition, a new row every time a value changes, and the output recorded in the final column. assets/images/ch2-trace-table.png ⚡ EXAM TIP — Trace Table Discipline (1) Only write a value in a column when it CHANGES — copying every value into every row invites mistakes. (2) Evaluate the loop condition one final time to show WHY the loop stops — examiners often allocate a mark to the final False. (3) Output goes in the output column ONLY when a print line actually runs. (4) Work with the values given in the question, never values you expect."
