    "heading": "2. Calculating Text File Sizes [Spec 3.2.2]",
    "id": "h-4",
    "text": "📋 SPEC 3.2.2 Be able to calculate the size of a text file: number of characters × bits per character. The formula is simple — the skill is being careful with units and remembering to count every character, including spaces and punctuation. TEXT FILE SIZE size (bits) = number of characters × bits per character size (bytes) = size in bits ÷ 8 WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — ASCII at one byte per character A text file contains 600 characters. Each character is stored in 1 byte. What is the file size in bytes and bits? 600 characters × 8 bits = 4,800 bits 4,800 ÷ 8 = 600 bytes (Shortcut: 1 byte per character → bytes = characters.) WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — 7-bit ASCII The sentence \"COMPUTER SCIENCE IS FUN\" is stored in 7-bit ASCII. Characters: 8 + 1 + 7 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 3 = 23 (count the spaces!) 23 × 7 = 161 bits 161 ÷ 8 = 20.125 → stored in 21 bytes (round UP to whole bytes) WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — working backwards A 7-bit ASCII file is 1,400 bits. How many characters? 1,400 ÷ 7 = 200 characters ⚡ EXAM TIP — Show Every Step Examiners award method marks . Write the formula, substitute the numbers, then do the arithmetic in clear stages (e.g. 23 × 7 = 161 because 20 × 7 = 140 and 3 × 7 = 21). A wrong final answer with correct working can still score most of the marks."
