    "heading": "7. Sound File Size Calculations [Spec 3.2.6]",
    "id": "h-12",
    "text": "📋 SPEC 3.2.6 Be able to calculate sound file sizes: sample rate × bit depth × duration. SOUND FILE SIZE (mono — one channel, assumed unless told otherwise) size (bits) = sample rate (Hz) × bit depth (bits) × duration (s) size (bytes) = size in bits ÷ 8 (For stereo, multiply by 2 — one data stream per channel.) WORKED EXAMPLE 1 A voice memo: sample rate 8,000 Hz, bit depth 8 bits, 10 seconds. Bits: 8,000 × 8 × 10 = 640,000 bits Bytes: 640,000 ÷ 8 = 80,000 bytes = 80 KB WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — larger numbers, still by hand A recording: sample rate 40,000 Hz, bit depth 16 bits, 30 seconds. Step 1: 40,000 × 16 = 640,000 bits per second Step 2: 640,000 × 30 = 19,200,000 bits Step 3: 19,200,000 ÷ 8 = 2,400,000 bytes = 2.4 MB WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — working backwards A mono file is 96,000 bits long, sampled at 4,000 Hz with a bit depth of 8. How long is the recording? Bits per second: 4,000 × 8 = 32,000 Duration: 96,000 ÷ 32,000 = 3 seconds ⚡ EXAM TIP — Break Big Multiplications into Stages Without a calculator, do one multiplication at a time and keep the zeros under control: 40,000 × 16 = 4 × 16 = 64, then add the four zeros → 640,000. Examiners expect this staged working — it also protects you from slips."
