    "heading": "8. Limitations of Binary Representation [Spec 3.2.7 & 3.2.8]",
    "id": "h-13",
    "text": "📋 SPEC 3.2.7 / 3.2.8 Understand the limitations of binary representations of data, including the trade-off between quality and file size, and that sampled data loses information. A digital file is always an approximation of the real, analogue world. Two things are lost when analogue data is digitised: Limitation Explanation Example Sampling loses what happens BETWEEN samples The wave is only measured at fixed intervals; any change in the sound between two samples is simply not recorded and can never be recovered A very short click between two samples disappears from the recording entirely Quantisation rounds each measurement Each sample must be stored in a fixed number of bits, so the true amplitude is rounded to the nearest available level — a small error in every single sample With 8-bit depth there are only 256 levels, so quiet detail is rounded away and the audio sounds \"grainy\" Pixels limit image detail A bitmap can only show detail as small as one pixel; anything finer is blended into a single colour Thin diagonal lines look jagged (\"staircased\") at low resolution Perfect quality is unaffordable Quality can always be improved by more samples/pixels/bits — but the file size grows in direct proportion, needing more storage and longer transfer times One minute of CD-quality stereo audio is over 10 MB uncompressed 📌 The Golden Sentence for 2-Mark Trade-Off Questions \"Increasing the (sample rate / bit depth / resolution / colour depth) makes the digital file a closer match to the original, improving quality (1) , but more bits must be stored, so the file size increases and it takes longer to transfer (1) .\" Adapt this sentence to whichever setting the question names."
