    "text": "Study how every calculation shows the formula, the substitution and staged arithmetic — that is what earns method marks. ✎ MODEL ANSWER [3 marks] Question: The ASCII code for the character 'C' is 67. (a) State the ASCII code for 'G'. (b) Explain how you worked out your answer. [3 marks] (a) The ASCII code for 'G' is 71 (1). (b) ASCII codes for letters are sequential — they run in alphabetical order (1). 'G' is four letters after 'C' in the alphabet, so its code is four more than 67: 67 + 4 = 71 (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ Correct value stated clearly ✓ The key property is named — codes are SEQUENTIAL/alphabetical — not just \"I counted\" ✓ The working is shown explicitly (67 + 4 = 71), which would still earn marks even if the addition had slipped ✎ MODEL ANSWER [4 marks] Question: An image is 150 pixels wide and 100 pixels high, with a colour depth of 8 bits. Calculate the file size of the image in kilobytes (KB). Show your working. [4 marks] File size in bits = width × height × colour depth (1) = 150 × 100 × 8 = 15,000 × 8 = 120,000 bits (1). Converting to bytes: 120,000 ÷ 8 = 15,000 bytes (1). Converting to kilobytes: 15,000 ÷ 1,000 = 15 KB (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ Formula stated before substituting — earns the method mark immediately ✓ Arithmetic broken into stages (150 × 100 first, then × 8) that are easy without a calculator ✓ Both unit conversions shown: ÷ 8 for bits→bytes, then ÷ 1,000 for bytes→KB (decimal units: 1 kB = 1,000 bytes; the binary kibibyte KiB = 1,024 bytes) ✓ Final answer given in the unit the question asked for ✎ MODEL ANSWER [4 marks] Question: A musician increases the sample rate of a recording from 8,000 Hz to 32,000 Hz. Explain the effect of this change on (a) the quality of the recording and (b) the file size. [4 marks] (a) The sound wave is now measured 32,000 times per second instead of 8,000, so the samples are much closer together (1). The stored data follows the shape of the original analogue wave more accurately, so less detail is lost between samples and the playback quality is higher (1). (b) Four times as many samples are taken every second, and each sample must be stored (1), so the file size becomes four times larger, meaning the file needs more storage space and takes longer to transfer (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ Quality answer explains the MECHANISM (samples closer together → wave tracked more accurately), not just \"better quality\" ✓ File size answer is quantified — 32,000 ÷ 8,000 = 4× — showing real understanding ✓ Consequence developed: more storage AND longer transfer ✓ Both halves of the question answered separately and labelled"
