    "heading": "6. Comparing Compression — Calculations [Spec 3.3.4]",
    "id": "h-8",
    "text": "Exam questions often give an original and a compressed size and ask you to compare them. Two ways to express the comparison: COMPRESSION CALCULATIONS saving (amount) = original size − compressed size compression ratio = original size ÷ compressed size WORKED EXAMPLE 1 A 12 MB bitmap is saved as a 3 MB JPEG. Saving: 12 − 3 = 9 MB (the file is 9 MB smaller) Ratio: 12 ÷ 3 = 4 (4 : 1 — one quarter of the original) WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — transfer time benefit A 40 MB file is compressed to 10 MB. The connection transfers 5 MB per second. Before: 40 ÷ 5 = 8 seconds After: 10 ÷ 5 = 2 seconds → transfer is 4× faster. WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — RLE saving A pixel row of 60 values compresses with RLE to 12 count–value pairs. If every value and every count takes 1 byte: Original: 60 × 1 = 60 bytes Compressed: 12 × 2 = 24 bytes Saving: 60 − 24 = 36 bytes Ratio: 60 ÷ 24 = 2.5 : 1"
