    "heading": "7. Encryption — Keeping Data Confidential [Spec 3.4.1]",
    "id": "h-9",
    "text": "🖼️ Image coming soon Figure 6.3 A Caesar cipher with key 3: each plaintext letter is shifted three places along the alphabet, wrapping from Z back round to A. assets/images/ch6-caesar-cipher.png 📋 SPEC 3.4.1 Understand that data can be encrypted to keep it secure, and the terms plaintext, ciphertext and key. Encryption scrambles data using an algorithm and a key so that anyone who intercepts it cannot understand it. Only someone with the correct key can decrypt it back to the original. Note carefully: encryption does not stop data being intercepted or stolen — it makes the stolen data meaningless. Term Meaning Example Plaintext The original, readable data before encryption HELLO Ciphertext The scrambled, unreadable data after encryption KHOOR Key The secret value used by the algorithm to encrypt and decrypt Shift of 3 Encrypt Convert plaintext → ciphertext using the algorithm and key HELLO + key 3 → KHOOR Decrypt Convert ciphertext → plaintext using the key (reverse the algorithm) KHOOR − key 3 → HELLO Why encrypt? Example Protect TRANSMITTED data — if messages are intercepted while crossing a network, the attacker sees only ciphertext Online banking and HTTPS websites; messaging apps Protect STORED data — if a device or drive is lost or stolen, the files cannot be read without the key An encrypted laptop drive; an encrypted customer database"
