    "heading": "10. Common Mistakes",
    "id": "h-17",
    "text": "✗ COMMON MISTAKES — DON'T DO THESE! ✗ Using 1,024 as the unit multiplier — Edexcel 4CP0 is decimal: 1 KB = 1,000 B, 1 MB = 1,000 KB, 1 GB = 1,000 MB, 1 TB = 1,000 GB. Always 1,000. ✗ Multiplying when you should divide (or vice versa) in unit conversions — converting to a LARGER unit makes the number SMALLER (divide); converting to a smaller unit makes the number bigger (multiply). Sanity-check the direction. ✗ Saying lossless compression \"loses a little bit of quality\" — lossless restores the original PERFECTLY. Any answer suggesting lossless degrades data scores zero for that point. ✗ Suggesting lossy compression for text, code or programs — losing characters corrupts meaning; losing bytes from a program breaks it. Lossy is only for images, sound and video. ✗ Forgetting that RLE can make files BIGGER — if the data has no runs, every value gains a count and the output grows. This is a favourite exam point. ✗ Decrypting a Caesar cipher by shifting FORWARDS — decryption shifts backwards by the key (or equivalently forwards by 26 − key). Always verify by re-encrypting. ✗ Forgetting the wrap-around — after Z the alphabet wraps to A (and going backwards, before A comes Z). Write out the alphabet to avoid miscounting. ✗ Claiming encryption stops data being intercepted — it does not. Interception can still happen; encryption makes the intercepted CIPHERTEXT unreadable without the key."
