   {
    "heading": "12. Quick Reference — Key Terms",
    "id": "h-22",
    "text": "★ QUICK REFERENCE — KEY TERMS Bit: A single binary digit, 0 or 1 — the smallest unit of data Nibble: 4 bits (one hexadecimal digit) Byte: 8 bits — typically one ASCII character Kilobyte / Megabyte / Gigabyte / Terabyte: 1 KB = 1,000 B; 1 MB = 1,000 KB; 1 GB = 1,000 MB; 1 TB = 1,000 GB — DECIMAL multiples in 4CP0 Compression: Re-encoding data so it needs fewer bits — saves storage, speeds transfer, uses less bandwidth Lossless compression: No data is discarded; the original is perfectly restored (RLE, ZIP, PNG). Required for text, code and programs Run-length encoding (RLE): Replacing runs of identical values with a count and the value (WWWWBBBWW → 4W 3B 2W). Ineffective — can even enlarge files — when there are no runs Lossy compression: Permanently discards data the user is unlikely to notice (JPEG, MP3). Much smaller files; original unrecoverable; never for text or programs Compression ratio: original size ÷ compressed size (e.g. 12 MB → 3 MB is 4 : 1) Encryption: Scrambling data with an algorithm and key so intercepted or stolen data is unreadable without the key Plaintext: The original readable data before encryption Ciphertext: The scrambled output of encryption Key: The secret value the algorithm uses to encrypt and decrypt Caesar cipher: Shift each letter a fixed number of places (the key), wrapping Z → A. Decrypt by shifting backwards Brute-force attack: Trying every possible key — trivial against Caesar's 25 keys Frequency analysis: Using letter frequencies (E is the most common in English) to deduce the key of a substitution cipher"
   }
