    "heading": "5. Main Memory — RAM, ROM & Cache [Spec 4.2.2]",
    "id": "h-8",
    "text": "Memory Volatile? Purpose RAM (Random Access Memory) Volatile (lost when power off) Holds the programs and data currently in use. The more RAM, the more can run at once without slowing down. ROM (Read Only Memory) Non-volatile Holds the startup (bootstrap) instructions that run first when the computer is switched on. Cannot normally be changed. Cache Volatile Very small, very fast memory inside/near the CPU holding the most frequently used data to speed up processing. 💡 Volatile vs non-volatile Volatile memory loses its contents when power is removed (RAM, cache). Non-volatile memory keeps its contents (ROM, secondary storage). 5.1 Virtual Memory [Spec 4.2.2] When RAM is full, the operating system can use part of secondary storage as virtual memory — an overflow area. Pages of data not currently needed are swapped out of RAM onto the disk and swapped back when required. ⚠️ The trade-off Virtual memory lets more programs run than physical RAM alone allows, but secondary storage is far slower than RAM, so heavy swapping (‘thrashing’) makes the computer feel sluggish. The long-term fix is more RAM."
