    "text": "🖼️ Image coming soon Figure 7.2 The three secondary storage technologies: magnetic (hard disk), optical (DVD) and solid state (SSD / flash). assets/images/ch7-storage-types.png Secondary storage is non-volatile and is needed to keep programs and files permanently (RAM cannot, because it is volatile and too small/expensive). Technology How it stores data Strengths Weaknesses Magnetic (HDD, tape) Magnetised areas on spinning platters/tape Very high capacity, low cost per GB Moving parts → slower, fragile, noisier Optical (CD/DVD/Blu-ray) Pits & lands read by a laser Cheap, portable, good for distribution Low capacity, slow, easily scratched Solid state (SSD, USB, SD) Flash memory chips — no moving parts Fast, durable, silent, low power More expensive per GB ⚡ EXAM TIP When choosing storage for a scenario, weigh up capacity, speed, portability, durability and cost per GB . E.g. a laptop for travel → SSD (durable, fast); a data-centre archive of huge backups → magnetic tape (cheap per GB). 6.1 Cloud Storage [Spec 4.2.6] Cloud storage means storing data on remote servers in data centres, accessed over the internet (e.g. Google Drive, OneDrive, iCloud). ✅ Advantages ⚠️ Disadvantages Access files from any device, anywhere No internet connection = no access Capacity can grow as needed (scalable) Ongoing subscription costs Provider handles backups and maintenance You must trust the provider’s security and privacy Easy sharing and collaboration Data travels over the internet — must be encrypted"
