    "text": "🖼️ Image coming soon Figure 11.3 Proprietary vs open-source licensing: closed source code with restrictive licence terms versus freely available source code that anyone may study, modify and redistribute. assets/images/ch11-licensing.png Intellectual property (IP) means creations of the mind — code, designs, music, inventions, brand names — that the creator owns and controls. A software licence is the legal agreement setting out what a user may do with a program. The two licensing models you must compare: Feature Proprietary (closed-source) Open-source Definition Source code kept secret; users buy a licence to USE the software only Source code made freely available; anyone may view, modify and redistribute it under the licence terms Examples Microsoft Windows, Microsoft Office, Adobe Photoshop Linux, Python, LibreOffice, Firefox, GIMP Cost Usually paid (one-off or subscription) Usually free of charge Modification Not allowed — code is hidden and legally protected Allowed and encouraged — software can be customised to exact needs Support Professional support from the vendor; guaranteed updates while supported Community forums and documentation; paid support exists for major projects but is not guaranteed Quality & security argument Dedicated paid developers; code secrecy hides flaws from attackers (but also from reviewers) 'Many eyes' — anyone can inspect the code, so bugs and security flaws can be found and fixed quickly Drawbacks Cost; locked to vendor's decisions ('vendor lock-in'); cannot fix or extend it yourself Variable documentation; usability can lag; no contractual guarantee anyone will fix your problem"
