    "heading": "7. Model Answers",
    "id": "h-19",
    "text": "Note how the 6-mark answer balances both sides and commits to a conclusion, and how legal answers name the exact law and year. ✎ MODEL ANSWER [3 marks] Question: Describe the three main offences under the Computer Misuse Act 1990. [3 marks] The first offence is unauthorised access to computer material — accessing programs or data without permission, such as logging into someone's account using a guessed password (1). The second is unauthorised access with intent to commit a further offence — for example, hacking into a system in order to commit fraud or theft (1). The third is unauthorised modification of computer material — changing or deleting data or programs without permission, which includes creating and distributing malware such as viruses or ransomware (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ All three offences named accurately and in order of seriousness ✓ Each offence is illustrated with a concrete example ✓ Correctly identifies that spreading malware falls under unauthorised modification — a detail examiners specifically look for. ✎ MODEL ANSWER [4 marks] Question: Explain how algorithmic bias can occur in a machine learning system used to shortlist job applicants. [4 marks] A machine learning system learns patterns from training data rather than being explicitly programmed (1). If the training data consists of the company's past hiring decisions, and those decisions favoured one group — for example, mostly male employees — the system learns to associate that group's characteristics with 'good candidates' (1). As a result, it systematically downgrades applications from under-represented groups, even though no programmer intended this (1). Because the system processes every application automatically and appears objective, the unfair decisions are applied at scale and may go unnoticed without testing for bias (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ Starts with the mechanism — ML learns from data — which is the root cause ✓ Traces the chain: biased historical data → learned association → unfair output ✓ Adds the 'scale and false objectivity' point, showing why algorithmic bias is more dangerous than one biased human ✓ Stays within the scenario (job applicants) throughout. ✎ MODEL ANSWER [6 marks] Question: A city council plans to install facial-recognition CCTV throughout the city centre. Discuss the benefits and drawbacks of this plan. [6 marks] One benefit is improved security: facial recognition can identify known offenders in real time, allowing police to respond quickly, and recorded footage provides evidence that helps convict criminals — which may also deter crime in the first place. A second benefit is efficiency: automated monitoring covers far more cameras than human operators could watch, so incidents are less likely to be missed. However, a major drawback is the loss of privacy: every innocent person in the city centre is scanned and potentially tracked without giving consent, creating a detailed record of their movements. A further drawback is algorithmic bias and error: facial-recognition systems have shown higher misidentification rates for some ethnic groups, so innocent people could be wrongly stopped, and such errors undermine public trust. There are also data protection concerns — the council must store and secure highly sensitive biometric data in line with the Data Protection Act 2018. Overall, while the security benefits are real, the plan is only justifiable if it is proportionate and tightly regulated — for example, used to search for specific suspects rather than to track everyone — because permanent mass surveillance of innocent people is too high a price for a general improvement in security. Why this scores full marks: ✓ Two developed benefits AND two developed drawbacks — genuinely balanced ✓ Uses precise vocabulary: facial recognition, algorithmic bias, biometric data, proportionate ✓ Brings in relevant law (DPA 2018) — cross-topic links earn the top level ✓ Conclusion takes a clear, justified position rather than sitting on the fence."
