    "heading": "10. Quick Reference — Key Terms & Revision",
    "id": "h-25",
    "text": "★ QUICK REFERENCE — KEY TERMS E-waste: Discarded electronic devices — contains toxic materials (lead, mercury, cadmium) and recoverable metals Planned obsolescence: Designing products to have a short useful life so customers replace them sooner Dematerialisation: Replacing physical products (CDs, paper, tickets) with digital equivalents Data centre: A facility full of servers powering cloud and internet services — a major consumer of electricity Privacy: An individual's right to control who collects, sees and uses information about them Surveillance: Monitoring of people's activity — CCTV, facial recognition, online monitoring Artificial intelligence (AI): Computers performing tasks that normally require human intelligence Machine learning (ML): Systems that learn patterns from training data instead of following explicitly programmed rules Training data: The example data a machine learning system learns from — its quality and representativeness determine the system's fairness Algorithmic bias: Systematically unfair automated decisions, usually caused by biased or unrepresentative training data Accountability: The question of who is responsible when an automated system causes harm Digital divide: The gap between those with access to devices, connectivity and digital skills, and those without Data Protection Act 2018: UK law (implementing GDPR) governing collection, storage and processing of personal data; gives individuals rights over their data Computer Misuse Act 1990: UK law criminalising: (1) unauthorised access; (2) unauthorised access with intent to commit further offences; (3) unauthorised modification, including malware Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988: UK law protecting original works — code, music, images, text — from unauthorised copying or distribution Intellectual property: Creations of the mind (code, designs, music, brands) owned and controlled by their creator Software piracy: Copying, distributing or using software without a valid licence Proprietary software: Closed-source software; users licence the right to use it but cannot view or modify the code (e.g. Windows, Photoshop) Open-source software: Software whose source code is freely available to view, modify and redistribute under its licence (e.g. Linux, Python, Firefox)"
